C = [
("h1", "5. Colossians 1:15–20 in Its Place"),
("p", "Claim: the passage on Christ in Colossians 1 is written to a church that is being offered something beside him. Paul answers by saying who he is, and the sentences about the image, the firstborn and the Creator are the answer. The volume reads them in the letter’s order, with the rivals of Colossians 2 in view."),
("sub", "5.1 The verses before the passage"),
("p", "Paul has prayed for the Colossians and given thanks. In 1:13 and 1:14 he speaks of the Father, “Who hath delivered us from the power of darkness, and hath translated us into the kingdom of his dear Son: In whom we have redemption through his blood, even the forgiveness of sins.” The next verse begins with “Who,” and the Son of 1:13 is the one it describes. The kingdom of his dear Son in 1:13 is not argued here. It is read in the volume on the Kingdom Program, and nothing in this volume depends on the reading."),
("sub", "5.2 The rivals of the second chapter"),
("p", "Paul says in 2:4 why he writes, and in 2:8 he names the danger: “Beware lest any man spoil you through philosophy and vain deceit, after the tradition of men, after the rudiments of the world, and not after Christ.” The things offered are philosophy, tradition and the rudiments of the world, and they are set against Christ. The passage in chapter 1 is not detached from that. Paul has already said in 2:2 and 2:3 that the mystery of God is “Christ; In whom are hid all the treasures of wisdom and knowledge.” Whoever offers a wisdom beside Christ is offering a thing that Christ already holds."),
("sub", "5.3 The passage clause by clause"),
("ctbl", "What Colossians 1:15–20 says and what it leaves unsaid.", [
    ("The clause", "What it says", "What it leaves unsaid"),
    ("“Who is the image of the invisible God”", "Christ is the image of the invisible God", "How an image of one who is invisible can be seen; the passage states the fact"),
    ("“the firstborn of every creature”", "He is the firstborn of every creature", "Whether the word names priority, rank or both; the verses that follow bear on it"),
    ("“For by him were all things created”", "All things were created by him, in heaven and earth, visible and invisible", "How his creating relates to the Father’s; the passage does not state it"),
    ("“whether they be thrones, or dominions, or principalities, or powers”", "The powers are among the things created", "Their rank, number or names; the verse lists four words"),
    ("“all things were created by him, and for him”", "By him and for him", "Why the creating was for him; the purpose is not given here"),
    ("“And he is before all things, and by him all things consist”", "He is before all things, and by him all things consist", "What “consist” means beyond holding together"),
    ("“And he is the head of the body, the church”", "He is the head of the body, the church", "How the Body’s head relates to the Creator of all things"),
    ("“the beginning, the firstborn from the dead”", "He is the beginning, the firstborn from the dead", "The order of the resurrections; that is a matter for the volume on the resurrection order"),
    ("“that in all things he might have the preeminence”", "The purpose is that in all things he might have the preeminence", "What the all things are; the passage uses the phrase twice"),
    ("“For it pleased the Father that in him should all fulness dwell”", "All fulness dwells in him, by the Father’s pleasure", "What the fulness is; chapter 8 takes it with Colossians 2:9"),
    ("“And, having made peace through the blood of his cross”", "He made peace through the blood of his cross", "The reconciliation of “all things”; left to the volume on the Cross"),
  ], [0.34, 0.33, 0.33]),
("sub", "5.4 The shape of the passage"),
("p", "The passage has two halves joined by the word “and.” The first (verses 15 to 17) is about creation: the image, the firstborn, the Creator, the one before all things. The second (verses 18 to 20) is about the church and the Cross: the head, the beginning, the firstborn from the dead, the fulness, the peace. The same person is the subject of both. A reader who takes only the first half sees a statement about the world. A reader who takes only the second sees a statement about the church. Paul gives both in one breath, and the volume reads them so."),
("p", "John Piper, in his sermon on the passage, reads it as the answer to false teaching: Paul means the clarity of Christ’s majesty to protect the Colossians from error, and he lists five ways Christ is supreme over creation and three over the church. The volume agrees with the use of Colossians 2 to read Colossians 1 and does not adopt the whole of his count."),
("sub", "5.5 The verses after the passage"),
("p", "The passage is followed at once by a turn to the readers: “And you, that were sometime alienated and enemies in your mind by wicked works, yet now hath he reconciled in the body of his flesh through death, to present you holy and unblameable and unreproveable in his sight” (Colossians 1:21, 22). The subject of “hath he reconciled” is the one just described. The one who is the image of the invisible God and by whom all things were created is the one who reconciled the Colossians in the body of his flesh. Paul does not break the line between the person of the first half and the work of the second. The condition that follows, “If ye continue in the faith grounded and settled” (Colossians 1:23), belongs to the volume on the Cross and to the study on reconciliation, and is not argued here. The point for this chapter is that the same subject runs from the image to the body of his flesh."),
("sub", "5.6 What the volume takes from the passage"),
("bul", [
    "The passage is set against philosophy, tradition and the rudiments of the world.",
    "Its first half speaks of creation and its second of the church and the Cross, with one subject.",
    "It names Christ as the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of every creature, the one by whom and for whom all things were created, and the one before all things.",
    "It does not define the image, does not rank the powers, and does not explain how the Creator is also the head of the church.",
  ]),
("p", "The next two chapters take the sentences about the image and the firstborn, and about the creating, and the one after takes the fulness."),
]
